#--: Overview

The declarations live in #~"lib/system/thread.z", each with its own doc
comment; this page is the prose and the worked examples.

The #~"thread" unit runs work on its own operating-system thread. The work is
a #~"Task" -- a class that declares it conforms (#~"task: thread.Task") and
has a #~"call" method. The task is #b:"moved" to the thread when it starts, so
nothing else can touch it while it runs, and #~"join" hands it back:

#code language=zerolang name=counter: """
  # Counter -- counts on its own thread
  Counter: class {
      # how far it counted
      n: i64
  } as {
      task: thread.Task
      call: function {:this} is {
          i: 0.i64
          for i < 1000 loop {
              this.n = this.n + 1
              i = i + 1
          }
      }
  }

  main: function is {
      h: thread.spawn task: (Counter n: 0) name: "counter"
      done: h.join
      print "counted \{done.n}"
  }
"""

There are no shared mutable variables to protect by hand: what a thread uses is
either moved to it or borrowed by it, and the lock model below keeps the owner
away from what a running task borrows. The #~"sync" unit (#~"Mutex",
#~"Channel" and the rest) is for data that really is shared.

#--: Tasks and handles

#ul:
  [#*: #b:"Task" -- #~"protocol { call: function {:this} }". The thread's body;
    it runs once. A task is any class that declares #~"task: thread.Task".]
  [#*: #b:"spawn" -- #~"function {task: T.take name: \"\" stackSize: 0} outx
    (JoinHandle T)". Starts the task. #~"name" labels the thread for
    debuggers and #~"top" (15 bytes are kept); a #~"stackSize" of #~"0" is the
    default, 8 MiB -- the main thread's usual size, so a program that recurses
    deeply on main recurses as deeply on a thread.]
  [#*: #b:"JoinHandle" -- the started thread. #~"join" waits for it and answers
    the task; #~"isFinished" says whether #~"join" would wait; #~"id" is the
    thread's id as the operating system numbers it (#~"0" once joined).
    #b:"Destroying a handle that was never joined joins it": a scope cannot end
    while a thread it started still uses what the scope owns.]
  [#*: #b:"spawnDetached" -- the same start with no handle: the thread runs to
    its end on its own and frees its task. The program may end while it still
    runs.]
  [#*: #b:"yieldNow" -- let another thread run.]
  [#*: #b:"running" -- how many threads the program started that have not
    finished. A thread leaves the count after its task is destroyed, so a
    program that must not end before its detached tasks waits for #~"0":
    #~"for thread.running > 0 loop thread.yieldNow".]

Read bare, a value whose type has a #~"call" is #b:"called" -- a task is an
iterator-shaped value in that respect -- so a task held in a variable is passed
with #~".take": #~"thread.spawn task: t.take".

#--: Borrowing tasks

A task may hold borrows -- a #~".borrow" field into data its owner keeps. The
handle carries the task's locks (#~"spawn" is a native #~"outx" function, the
one kind of call that may keep a moved value's locks), so while the handle
lives, or the task #~"join" answered, the owner can neither write nor read nor
drop what the task borrows, and two running tasks cannot borrow one source:

#code language=zerolang name=summer: """
  # Summer -- sums a list its owner lends it
  Summer: class {
      # the borrowed input
      xs: (ListVal i64).borrow
      # the answer
      total: i64
  } as {
      task: thread.Task
      call: function {:this} is {
          for each x: this.xs loop this.total = this.total + x
      }
  }

  main: function is {
      xs: ListVal i64
      xs.append 1
      xs.append 2
      {
          h: thread.spawn task: (Summer :xs total: 0)
          s: h.join
          print "summed \{s.total}"
      }
      xs.append 3
      print "the owner writes again: \{xs.length}"
  }
"""

Inside the inner block #~"xs.append 3" is refused (#~"exclusive lock held by
'h'"); after it, the lock is gone. #~"join" is #~"{:this} outx T": the task it
answers keeps the handle locked, which is what keeps the task's borrows alive.

#~"spawnDetached" is #~"out", not #~"outx": nothing would keep what a detached
task borrows alive, so a task holding a borrow is refused there.

#--: What may cross to a thread

A task moves to its thread whole, with everything it holds. A type that
conforms to #~"thread.ThreadBound" -- a marker protocol, with no methods --
never makes that move: a value of it is refused as a task, and as anything a
task holds at any depth, whether it is owned, viewed or borrowed, or sits in a
list inside another class. The lock guards (#~"sync.MutexGuard",
#~"sync.ReadGuard", #~"sync.WriteGuard") are the stdlib's: a guard unlocks on
the thread that locked, and unlocked on another the lock is undefined. Lock on
the new thread instead -- the task holds the Mutex, by #~".view", and takes the
guard itself:

#code language=zerolang expect=E0200: """
  # Waiter -- waits on a guard its owner locked
  Waiter: class {
      # the guard, locked on the owner's thread
      g: sync.MutexGuard (ListVal i64)
  } as {
      task: thread.Task
      call: function {:this} is {
          print "\{this.g.value.length}"
      }
  }

  main: function is {
      m: (sync.Mutex (ListVal i64)).create value: ListVal i64
      g: m.lock
      h: thread.spawn task: (Waiter :g)
      h.join
  }
"""

Refused: "Cannot hand 'Waiter' to another thread with #~"thread.spawn": it
holds 'MutexGuard', and a #~"thread.ThreadBound" value never leaves the thread
that made it".

#--: Panics

A panic on any thread ends the whole program, with status #~"1": the panic is
reported once, however many threads panic, and the process ends without running
exit handlers under the other threads' feet. There is no catching it and no
poisoned state to recover.

#--: Building

A program that starts a thread links #~"pthread" (the #~"zlink:" line of its C
says so), and its C differs from a program that does not only where threads
need it -- the panic above. Programs that start no thread compile exactly as
they did. The C layer is #~"src/runtime/natives/_Z_THREAD_SHIM.inc": pthreads
on Linux and other POSIX systems, Win32 threads on Windows.
